+It opens your browser once at the Microsoft sign-in page, receives the response on `127.0.0.1`, and
+stores the result in your Keychain. **You will see a consent screen listing exactly the three
+read-only permissions from Step 5 — read it, and if it lists anything else, stop and say so.**
 
+Before the browser opens, RichOS prints what it is about to ask for and which directory it is asking
+in, so a mistyped tenant is something you catch here rather than after approving a screen:
+
+```
+account:    you@yourcompany.com
+client:     <your Application (client) ID>  (yours — RichOS ships no OAuth client of its own)
+tenant:     <your Directory (tenant) ID>  (your own directory — never "/common")
+requesting: 3 read-only scopes:
+            https://graph.microsoft.com/Calendars.Read   (Calendar)
+            https://graph.microsoft.com/Files.Read        (OneDrive)
+            https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.ReadBasic    (Outlook)
+```
+
+Leave `--source` off and RichOS asks for whatever that account asked for last time — the first time,
+that is Calendar only. Naming the sources is how you widen or narrow it, and re-running the command
+for the same account re-consents rather than adding anything.
+
+**If RichOS reports `NOT CONNECTED` with an `AADSTS…` code about a client secret,** your registration
+is not actually a public client. That is Step 4 above, and it is one switch:
+**Entra → your app → Authentication → Advanced settings → "Allow public client flows" = Yes → Save**.
+Run the command again; you do not need to create a secret. The refusal prints those words too, so you
+do not have to come back here for them. (If your organization genuinely forbids public client flows,
+the refusal names the other way through, and RichOS keeps that secret in your Keychain and in no
+file.)
+
+After that, `workspace status microsoft` shows what RichOS can see, and
+`workspace sync microsoft --once` pulls it:
+
+```
+richos-service workspace status microsoft
+richos-service workspace sync microsoft --once
+```
+
+### More than one Microsoft 365 account
+
+Run the same command again with a different `--account`. It **adds** that account — its own consent
+screen, its own Keychain entry, its own sync position — and every account already connected is left
+exactly as it was; RichOS names them on a `keeping:` line before the browser opens so you can see
+that for yourself. The app registration and the tenant are shared, because they are your one app in
+your one directory.
+
+`status` and `sync` then cover every connected account unless you name one with `--account`.
+`disconnect` needs `--account` once two are connected, and will not pick one for you.
+
