 ## Things worth knowing afterwards
 
+**What a sync prints.** Pulling is only half of it — the pull ends by promoting what it found into
+loro's memory, which is what makes it answerable later:
+
+```
+calendar:   observed 2, ingested 2, deduped 0
+evidence:   ~/RichOS/corpus/ceo/unfiled/evidence/workspace
+promoted:   2 events into memory, 2 people learned, 0 items held
+memory:     ~/RichOS/corpus
+```
+
+Anything held is listed with the reason it was held, never as a bare number. `--no-promote` pulls
+without promoting, for when you want to see what a sync would fetch and nothing else; it says so on
+the same line rather than quietly doing less.
+
 **Where your tokens live.** In your Mac's Keychain, under `com.richos.workspace.microsoft`, encrypted
 at rest by macOS — never in a file in any RichOS folder. You can see the entry in Keychain Access.
 
+**Where your two IDs live.** In `_oauth_client_microsoft.json` inside your corpus — your client ID,
+your tenant, the loopback redirect and the list of accounts you have connected, each with the
+permissions it asked for. Neither ID is a secret and there is no secret in that file. It is a separate
+file from the Google one, so connecting one vendor cannot disturb the other.
+
 **How to disconnect.** `workspace disconnect microsoft` deletes those tokens, and from that moment
 this machine cannot read anything of yours. It will also tell you, plainly, that **the consent record
 in your Microsoft account is still there** — Microsoft gives an app no way to revoke its own
